                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Decode the brief, find the real problem`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The brief is one page, ambiguous and over-scoped: <strong>11 topics, 4 days, $280K, 8 weeks</strong> — and those four don't fit together. Read the intake call and the participant data, then separate the CHRO's <em>stated wants</em> from the <em>underlying business problem</em>: clinical experts failing the jump to people management, driving manager retention and patient-safety risk. Use <strong>backward design</strong> — start from the 3–4 outcomes that actually move that problem, not the wish list. The trap is topic-stuffing all 11 because the client asked: that's 35 minutes per topic and a program that fails her real need.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Configure the program inside the constraints`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Assemble the cohort from the module library under the <strong>iron triangle</strong>: you cannot maximize breadth, speed and low cost at once. The hard caps are <strong>≤4 classroom days</strong> (every day is a clinician off the ward) and <strong>≤8 weeks to launch</strong>. Choose a delivery mix, decide <strong>build-vs-reuse</strong> for each module — reuse cuts cost and lead-time but dilutes the “built-for-you” feel — and resolve the <em>faculty gap</em>: 3 of the 5 expected faculty are committed elsewhere, including marquee Dr. Alvarez (in Singapore until Week 7). Wait, substitute, or anchor with available seniors plus an external practitioner.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Price the engagement`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Build the engagement P&L. You must hold a <strong>contribution margin of ≥25%</strong>, stay at or under the <strong>$280K ceiling</strong>, and beat the competitor's $240K off-the-shelf value. Learn the distinction that separates pros from amateurs: <em>cutting price</em>, <em>cutting cost</em> and <em>cutting scope</em> are three different moves with three different consequences for margin and for renewal. Choose a pricing posture, set the price, pick the lever you pull when margin is tight, and decide how much delivery happens on-site at the hospital for shift workers.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the proposal to the CHRO`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The CHRO presses on the three tensions in a 3-minute defense: <em>“Why did you drop topics I asked for?”</em>, <em>“Your competitor is $40K cheaper”</em>, and <em>“Can you really launch in 8 weeks?”</em> Defend your scope decisions by tying them to the <strong>business outcome</strong> — manager retention and patient safety — name in one sentence what your price premium buys, and commit to delivery milestones reconciled with faculty lead-times. Then decide the audition question: how much first-cohort margin would you sacrifice to win the <strong>$3.2M renewal</strong> — without ever going below the 25% floor?`,

                    r1_problem_label:       `What is MeridianHealth's real problem behind the one-page wish list?`,
                    r1_scope_label:         `How do you treat the 11 requested topics against your diagnosis?`,
                    r1_questions_label:     `Before designing, how rigorously do you go back to the client with the right questions? (%)`,
                    r1_questions_readout:   `Discovery rigor before you design:`,
                    prob_transition:        `Clinical experts failing the jump to people management — a retention and patient-safety risk`,
                    prob_knowledge:         `The managers lack technical knowledge — they need 11 topics of content`,
                    prob_prestige:          `The CHRO wants a prestigious, comprehensive academy to impress her board`,
                    prob_compliance:        `A compliance gap — managers must be certified across every listed subject`,
                    scope_core:             `Prioritize to a core 3–4 outcomes that move the diagnosed problem; mark the rest nice-to-have or out of scope`,
                    scope_all:              `Build all 11 — the client asked for them, so deliver the full list`,
                    scope_half:             `Cram 7–8 topics as a compromise to look responsive`,

                    r2_format_label:        `Choose the delivery mix that fits ≤4 classroom days and ≤8 weeks`,
                    r2_reuse_label:         `Build-vs-reuse: how much of the program is genuinely custom-built?`,
                    r2_faculty_label:       `Resolve the faculty gap — 3 of the 5 expected faculty are unavailable, incl. marquee Dr. Alvarez`,
                    r2_classroom_label:     `Classroom days committed (client cap is 4)`,
                    r2_classroom_hint:      `Every classroom day is a clinician off the ward. The CHRO was explicit: 4 days maximum.`,
                    format_blended:         `Blended journey — 3 classroom days + virtual sessions, coaching circles and on-the-job assignments between shifts`,
                    format_classroom:       `All classroom — 6 immersive days to fit the full learning journey`,
                    format_virtualonly:     `Almost entirely virtual — 1 classroom day to save cost and time`,
                    reuse_hybrid:           `Custom the few modules that carry the “built-for-you” feel; reuse the rest invisibly`,
                    reuse_allcustom:        `Build everything from scratch — maximum customization at maximum cost and lead-time`,
                    reuse_allreuse:         `Reuse the catalog almost entirely — cheapest and fastest, off-the-shelf feel`,
                    faculty_mix:            `Anchor with available senior faculty + a healthcare practitioner partner; sequence Dr. Alvarez's module to a later week`,
                    faculty_wait:           `Wait for Dr. Alvarez and the full marquee roster — they're back in Week 7`,
                    faculty_substitute:     `Substitute junior associates across the board — cheapest, but a visible drop in client perception`,

                    r3_posture_label:       `Choose your pricing posture against the $240K competitor and the $280K ceiling`,
                    r3_price_label:         `Set the engagement price ($k) — ceiling is $280k`,
                    r3_price_hint:          `Cutting price protects the win but eats margin; cutting cost or scope protects margin differently.`,
                    r3_lever_label:         `If margin is tight, which lever do you pull?`,
                    r3_onsite_label:        `Deliver on-site at the hospital rather than on-campus? (% of sessions on-site)`,
                    r3_onsite_readout:      `On-site delivery (raises cost, raises fit for shift workers):`,
                    posture_premium:        `Hold near $280K and justify the premium with named customization (coaching circles, on-site, healthcare simulation)`,
                    posture_match:          `Match the competitor at $240K to win on price`,
                    posture_phased:         `Propose a phased deal — a paid pilot now, scale on renewal`,
                    posture_undercut:       `Undercut at $215K to guarantee the win`,
                    lever_cutcost:          `Cut cost invisibly — tighten logistics and faculty mix without touching the client-visible design`,
                    lever_valueadd:         `Add value to justify price — a coaching circle and on-site delivery the competitor can't match`,
                    lever_cutscope:         `Cut scope — drop a core module to hit the number`,
                    lever_cutprice:         `Just cut price further and absorb the thinner margin`,

                    r4_topic_label:         `The CHRO: “Why did you drop topics I asked for?” How do you answer?`,
                    r4_price_label:         `The CHRO: “Your competitor is $40K cheaper.” How do you handle it?`,
                    r4_timeline_label:      `The CHRO: “Can you really launch in 8 weeks?” What do you commit?`,
                    r4_renewal_label:       `How much first-cohort margin would you sacrifice to win the $3.2M renewal? (% margin you'd hold above the floor)`,
                    r4_renewal_hint:        `The audition logic: a thinner-but-genuine first cohort can be worth more than a fat one-off margin — but never below the 25% floor.`,
                    topic_outcome:          `Tie every kept module to manager retention and patient safety; show 35 min/topic would have failed her real problem`,
                    topic_apologize:        `Apologize and offer to add the dropped topics back into the 4 days`,
                    topic_blame:            `Say the budget forced the cuts — it's a money problem, not a design choice`,
                    price_differentiate:    `Name in one sentence what the $40K buys: healthcare-specific simulation, coaching circles, on-site for shift workers`,
                    price_discount:         `Drop your price to match — protect the win at any margin`,
                    price_prestige:         `Say “we're BEL” — the brand justifies the premium`,
                    timeline_milestones:    `Commit to named delivery milestones reconciled with faculty lead-times — and flag what we sequence later`,
                    timeline_promise:       `Promise the full program live in 8 weeks, no caveats — figure out the build later`,
                    timeline_hedge:         `Hedge — “we'll do our best” with no committed dates or owners`,

                    warn_questions_range:   `Discovery rigor must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_classroom_range:   `Classroom days must be a whole number between 1 and 8.`,
                    warn_price_range:       `The engagement price must be a sensible figure (between $100k and $400k).`,
                    warn_onsite_range:      `On-site delivery must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_renewal_range:     `Margin-sacrifice willingness must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_problem_right: `<strong>That's the real diagnosis.</strong> Her 11 topics are symptoms; the problem is that brilliant clinicians are drowning as first-time managers, and that bleeds retention and patient safety. Design backward from <em>that</em> and you'll know which topics earn a place.`,
                    coach_r1_knowledge:     `<strong>You've taken the wish list at face value.</strong> These managers aren't short on clinical knowledge — they're failing the jump to leading people. Eleven topics of content won't fix a management-transition problem. What outcome actually moves manager retention?`,
                    coach_r1_prestige:      `<strong>Careful — you're designing for the CHRO's vanity, not her managers.</strong> A comprehensive academy that impresses a board but doesn't change how 48 people manage is exactly the over-scoped trap. The audition is won by results, not breadth.`,
                    coach_r1_compliance:    `<strong>This isn't a certification exercise.</strong> Framing it as compliance pushes you straight back to building all 11 boxes to tick. The real risk is clinical experts failing as managers — diagnose the behavior change, not a checklist.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp work.</strong> You named the underlying problem — the management transition — AND you refused to topic-stuff, prioritizing to a core 3–4. That is backward design: the program now serves her real need instead of her wish list.`,
                    coach_r1_stuff:         `<strong>You've built her wish list and failed her problem.</strong> Eleven topics in four days is 35 minutes each — nobody leads a team better because they got 35 minutes on hospital finance. Which 3 topics move manager retention, and why did you let the list override your diagnosis?`,
                    coach_r1_half:          `<strong>Seven or eight topics is still topic-stuffing in a nicer suit.</strong> A compromise that looks responsive isn't the same as a program designed backward from the outcome. Cut harder — to the 3–4 that actually change how these managers lead.`,
                    coach_r1_noquestions:   `<strong>You're designing blind.</strong> The brief is deliberately ambiguous; designing without going back to the client with the right questions guarantees you build to the wrong target. Discovery is cheap now and ruinous to skip.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>A blended journey respects the iron triangle.</strong> Three classroom days plus virtual sessions, coaching circles and on-the-job work fits the 4-day cap and reaches clinicians between shifts — the outcome delivered inside the client's operational reality.`,
                    coach_r2_classroom:     `<strong>Six immersive classroom days is a beautiful program you can't release the people for.</strong> Every day is a clinician off the ward, and the CHRO said four maximum. How do you deliver the outcome inside the constraint — virtual, coaching between shifts, on-the-job?`,
                    coach_r2_virtual:       `<strong>Going almost fully virtual saves cost but guts the experience.</strong> First-time managers learning to lead need practice and peer interaction; a webinar series feels off-the-shelf and won't build the “built-for-you” conviction the renewal rests on.`,
                    coach_r2_overdays:      `<strong>You've exceeded the 4-day classroom cap.</strong> The managers are clinicians — every classroom day is a day off the ward, and the CHRO was explicit. A program they can't release the people for doesn't get delivered, however good it looks.`,
                    coach_r2_allcustom:     `<strong>Building everything from scratch blows the lead-time.</strong> Maximum customization sounds client-pleasing, but you have 8 weeks, not 14, and three key faculty are unavailable. Custom the few modules that carry the feel; reuse the rest invisibly.`,
                    coach_r2_allreuse:      `<strong>That's the off-the-shelf academy the competitor already sells — for less.</strong> Reusing the catalog wholesale is cheap and fast, but the CHRO can tell it isn't built for her, and a generic cohort loses the audition no matter the price.`,
                    coach_r2_wait:          `<strong>Waiting for the full marquee roster breaks your timeline.</strong> Dr. Alvarez is in Singapore until Week 7 and you promised Week 8 — you've designed a date you can't hit. What do you sequence later or substitute so the launch still lands?`,
                    coach_r2_substitute:    `<strong>All-junior substitution is the cheapest and the most visible.</strong> The client expected senior names; a wall of associates reads as “repackaged from the catalog” and quietly tells the CHRO this isn't the custom program she's auditioning.`,
                    coach_r2_realistic:     `<strong>This is a deliverable design.</strong> A blended journey inside the 4-day cap, anchored by available seniors and a healthcare practitioner with Dr. Alvarez sequenced later — you've made the iron triangle explicit instead of discovering it at delivery.`,
                    coach_r3_overceiling:   `<strong>You're over the $280K ceiling.</strong> The CHRO's board sees a number above her stated budget before they read a word of your design. Whatever the justification, pricing above the hard cap loses credibility on the first slide.`,
                    coach_r3_lossmaking:    `<strong>This engagement loses money.</strong> Your price is below your delivery cost — a negative margin. You'd win the audition and bleed cash on it. How do you take a loss-making cohort into a 5-year renewal conversation?`,
                    coach_r3_belowfloor:    `<strong>You're below the 25% margin floor.</strong> You may have matched the competitor, but a thin margin means the won cohort doesn't fund the unit — and you haven't counted the custom redesign you promised. Did you cut price when you should have cut cost?`,
                    coach_r3_healthy:       `<strong>That's a disciplined P&L.</strong> A margin at or above the 25% floor, under the ceiling — the engagement actually funds the unit. Now make sure the price has a one-sentence justification the CHRO can repeat to her board.`,
                    coach_r3_overpriced:    `<strong>A fat margin on the audition can backfire.</strong> Above ~45% on a first cohort you're optimizing this deal over the relationship; the CHRO may read it as gouging the very engagement that's meant to earn her trust. Sometimes you invest in the renewal.`,
                    coach_r3_match:         `<strong>Matching at $240K can work — if the math does.</strong> Beating the competitor on price only helps if your cost stack still clears the 25% floor. Match the price, but protect the margin by cutting cost, not scope.`,
                    coach_r3_phased:        `<strong>A phased deal is a smart hedge.</strong> A paid pilot now that scales on renewal de-risks the timeline and the budget for both sides — provided each phase still carries a healthy margin.`,
                    coach_r3_undercut:      `<strong>Undercutting to guarantee the win usually guarantees a loss.</strong> Pricing below the competitor torches your margin and signals you're commoditized. You don't win a custom audition by being the cheapest box on the page.`,
                    coach_r3_cutcost:       `<strong>Cutting cost invisibly is the professional's move.</strong> Tightening logistics and faculty mix protects the client-visible design and the margin at once — the difference between cutting cost and cutting price.`,
                    coach_r3_valueadd:      `<strong>Adding value to justify the price is exactly the premium play.</strong> A coaching circle and on-site delivery the competitor can't match give the CHRO a one-sentence reason to pay more — that's how you're premium, not just expensive.`,
                    coach_r3_cutscope:      `<strong>Cutting a core module to hit a number guts the program.</strong> You've protected the price by removing the very outcome that moves manager retention — that's cutting scope dressed up as cutting cost, and the CHRO will feel the hole.`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>That's how you defend a dropped topic.</strong> Tying every kept module to manager retention and patient safety shows you said no <em>for</em> her managers, not to her. “35 minutes on finance wouldn't have helped them lead” is an answer a CHRO respects.`,
                    coach_r4_apologize:     `<strong>Apologizing and cramming the topics back in undoes your design.</strong> You diagnosed the real problem in Round 1 — now you're surrendering it under pressure and re-stuffing four days. Defend the cut on the outcome, don't fold.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the budget makes the cuts look arbitrary.</strong> “We couldn't afford it” tells the CHRO your design wasn't a choice — it was a constraint you resented. Frame the prioritization as deliberate backward design, not a money excuse.`,
                    coach_r4_differentiate: `<strong>One sentence, and you're premium.</strong> Naming what the $40K buys — a healthcare-specific simulation, coaching circles, on-site delivery for shift workers — turns a price gap into a value gap. The competitor's off-the-shelf academy can't say that.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>You discounted under pressure.</strong> Dropping price to match re-opens the margin wound you just closed — and teaches the client that your number was soft. If the value is real, defend it; don't give away the margin at the table.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>“We're BEL” is not a value argument.</strong> The board sees two line items and one is cheaper; the brand doesn't fill a $40K gap. Name the customization that justifies the premium, or you're not premium — you're just expensive.`,
                    coach_r4_promise:       `<strong>Promising the full program in 8 weeks with no caveats is how you over-deliver on stress and under-deliver on quality.</strong> The simulation needs build time and a key faculty member is away until Week 7. Commit to milestones you can actually hit.`,
                    coach_r4_hedge:         `<strong>“We'll do our best” is not a commitment.</strong> A CHRO auditioning you for a $3.2M renewal needs named milestones and owners, not a hedge. Vague timelines read as “they're not sure they can deliver.”`,
                    coach_r4_audition:      `<strong>That's the audition logic done right.</strong> You held a genuine margin above the floor while signaling you'll invest in the relationship — not gouging the first cohort, not gutting it either. That's how a thin-but-real audition wins a fat renewal.`,
                    coach_r4_oneoff:        `<strong>You optimized this cohort and forgot the relationship.</strong> A fat margin with no willingness to invest in the audition wins $280K and risks the $3.2M renewal. When is it worth a thinner margin to win a multi-year client?`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
